## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: b83ce94c63ec1891f5b76837455c8b2a53ad9d9c

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I examined the fix diff first: it splits a single combined `if` condition into two separate `if` blocks that each set their own "already played" flag (`m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove` / `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove`). I then used `git log --follow -p` on `TripleAPlayer.java` to trace the history of the `move()` sound-playing block through time and located the commit that first introduced the merged/buggy condition.

**(2) The introducing change:** Commit `b83ce94c6` ("Dan Van Atta", Sep 18 2015) refactored the sound system from `DefaultSoundChannel.playSoundOnLocalMachine(...)` to `ClipPlayer.play(...)`. During that refactor it collapsed what was previously correct logic:
```java
if (nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) {
    ... CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT ...
    m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
} else if (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove) {
    ... CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_COMBAT ...
    m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true;
}
```
into a single merged condition:
```java
if ((nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove) || (!nonCombat && !m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove)) {
    ClipPlayer.play(SoundPath.CLIP_PHASE_MOVE_NONCOMBAT, id.getName());
    m_soundPlayedAlreadyNonCombatMove = true;
}
```
This dropped the `m_soundPlayedAlreadyCombatMove = true;` assignment entirely (and the distinct combat-move sound clip), so during a combat move phase the "already played" flag for combat moves was never set, causing the move sound to replay on every step of a combat move (and always playing the non-combat clip instead of the combat one).

**(3) Supporting evidence:** `git log --follow -p` shows this exact merged-condition code persisting unchanged from `b83ce94c6` through subsequent history until `9a02e14e1` (the given bug-fixing commit), which restores the two independent `if` blocks with separate flag assignments — functionally reverting the flawed merge from `b83ce94c6` (though the fix commit itself still uses the non-combat clip for both branches, a separate/secondary issue not part of the original flag bug).

I'm confident in this identification — the fix commit's change maps directly and exclusively onto the logic altered in `b83ce94c6`, with no other intervening commits touching this code block.

